1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.nlb.gov.sg/biblioasia/2017/01/05/1960s-fashion-the-legacy-of-made-to-measure/
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1965, realising that jeans were on the cusp of becoming ubiquitous for the younger generation, Eunice Wong ventured into a unisex '''Jeans East West (Jeans' Boutique East West / Jeans West)''' shop at 104 Orchard Road (near the
2 CONC  Lee & Fletcher head office at the Concorde Hotel) with her son, Phila Mae’s brother, '''Vincent'''. He used to make his own tie-dye T-shirts to sell with the California West brand of jeans that they stocked. “All his girlfriends 
2 CONC were slaves,” Eunice Wong recounts, “they’d do tie-dye T-shirts for his shop. Their target market was rich kids who could afford to pay $30 for jeans at that time, it was a lot of money”.
2 CONT
